lity of 70B on available hardware (2xGPU vs CPU impracticality)", "Whether quantization+rotation overhead dominates at 7B vs memory savings at 70B — the empirical hypothesis to test", "How much KV-4-bit decode benefit scales with context length (tested via Arm5-vs-Arm3 curve)", "Representativeness of the sampled perplexity-gate subset for the 70B sanity check and the validity of the chosen +2.0 PPL threshold", "Kernel-fidelity of the GPU path if the paper's 4-bit kernel library isn't exactly reproduced", "Transfer validity of CPU-only decode numbers to GPU"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-7308659a0a8e4dbb9fc5a38018ceabd1", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-7308659a0a8e4dbb9fc5a38018ceabd1", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"stage": "result", "summary": "Deliver complete experiment design for QuaRot prefill/decode comparison", "answer": "# Complete experiment design (design-only; no experiment claimed or executed)\n\n## 0. Fixed scope and what is NOT in the workspace (finding, verified by inspection)\n- `/workspace` contains only `task.json`; `track.md` = \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n- No QuaRot code/repo, no model configs, no datasets, no measurement scripts anywhere on the filesystem.\n- Environment facts: no GPU (`/dev/nvidia*` absent); 224 CPUs; ~2.0 TiB RAM; Python 3.14.4 with **no torch/transformers/quant packages installed**; network disabled; no bundled datasets.\n- Consequence: **every QuaRot technical detail is an assumption from the public QuaRot paper, not a documented fact.** The experiment contract question and the sandbox environment are fixed requirements; QuaRot pipeline specifics, external checkpoints, external datasets, and GPU hardware are assumptions (enumerated in \u00a77 and \u00a78).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Full run matrix\n\n### 1.1 Dimensions\n- **Models (3):** LLaMa-2 7B, 13B, 70B. All three \u2014 7B/13B probe the regime where quantization+rotation overheads are relatively large; 70B is the decisive memory-bandwidth-bound case and QuaRot's headline scale. (Feasibility per size in \u00a76.)\n- **Arms (5 core + 1 optional):**\n  1. `FP16` \u2014 off-the-shelf pretrained, no rotation, no quantization (**global baseline**).\n  2. `R-W16A16` \u2014 rotated, all FP16 (**isolates rotation overhead** vs Arm 1).\n  3. `R-W4A4KV16` \u2014 rotated, 4-bit weights + 4-bit activations, 16-bit KV (**no KV quant**).\n  4. `R-W4A16KV4` \u2014 rotated, 4-bit weights, 16-bit activations, 4-bit KV (**no activation quant**).\n  5. `R-W4A4KV4` \u2014 full QuaRot end-to-end 4-bit (**treatment**).\n  6. `OPT-W8A8KV8` \u2014 optional secondary 8-bit reference, cost permitting (adds an independent integer-baseline; marked optional, dropped if budget-constrained).\n- **Prefill prompt lengths (6):** L \u2208 {128, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096} (within LLaMa-2's 4K context). Unbatched prefill primary; batched prefill (batch=8 at L=1024) secondary.\n- **Decode:** fixed `max_new_tokens = 128` per run, **greedy decoding** (temperature=0, fixed seeds) so token trajectories and KV growth match across arms; decode batch \u2208 {1 (primary), 8 (secondary)}.\n- **Repetitions:** prefill \u22655 timed runs; decode \u226510 timed runs (per-token steps are short \u2192 cheap to repeat); memory captured once per config after warm-up; final reported value = **median** with spread (IQR, or std when n\u226510).\n- **Realism probe (secondary):** a fixed small set (\u226416 sequences) of real prompts drawn from CNN/DailyMail (summarization-style) or WikiText-2 at L=1024, to confirm controlled-length sweeps represent real workloads. These datasets are **not bundled** \u2192 must be supplied or the probe is dropped and synthetic prompts cover it (assumption \u00a77).\n- **Seeds:** one global seed per model; prompts generated/sampled once with that seed and reused verbatim across all arms \u2192 identical inputs per cell.\n\n### 1.2 Run-count summary (planning figures, not results)\n- Prefill timing cells: 3 models \u00d7 5 arms \u00d7 6 L = 90 cells \u00d7 5 reps = 450 timing runs (+ optional batched prefill 3\u00d75\u00d71\u00d75 = 75) .\n- Decode timing cells: 3 models \u00d7 5 arms \u00d7 6 L \u00d7 {batch 1, batch 8} = 180 cells \u00d7 10 reps = 1 800 short runs.\n- Perplexity gate: WikiText-2 subset (fixed seed, e.g., 128 held-out sequences) \u00d7 (FP16 + 5 arms) \u00d7 {7B, 13B}; 70B gate is a smaller sampled subset or, if too costly, reported as a reduced sanity check (assumption \u00a77).\n- Optional W8A8 arm multiplies the above grids by 1/6 \u2014 included only if budget allows.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Latency and memory measurement protocol\n\n### 2.1 GPU path (primary hardware)\n- Fixed device selection; CUDA events (`torch.cuda.Event`) bracket each timed region; `torch.cuda.synchronize()` before start and after end; `time.perf_counter()` as an independent cross-check.\n- **Warm-up:** 2 full (prefill+decode or prefill-only) passes per cell before timing \u2014 excludes lazy init/kernel compilation/page-faults.\n- **Repetition and reporting:** \u22655 prefill reps / \u226510 decode reps per cell; report **median**; trim outliers (>3\u00d7IQR) as a documented rule; report spread.\n- **Memory:** `torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()` peak deltas around prefill and around decode windows + explicit component accounting (weights + KV + activations, see \u00a73.4). Memory is reported per configuration, not per rep.\n- Fixed decode hyper-parameters (batch, N tokens, temperature=0, seed) across all arms; same prompt tensors.\n\n### 2.2 CPU fallback path (explicitly labeled, not passed off as GPU results)\n- `torch.set_num_threads(224)`, MKL/OpenMP thread pinning documented; `time.perf_counter()` with barrier-equivalent synchronization via `torch` ops; same warm-up/median/outlier rules.\n- 4-bit arms on CPU are **emulations** (weights+K+V stored at 4-bit, GEMM run in FP16/FP32 with CPU BLAS): measured numbers are labeled \"CPU-reference only \u2014 no Marlin/GPU 4-bit kernels; decode is DRAM-bandwidth bound; factors do not transfer to GPU.\"\n\n### 2.3 Quality-gate protocol (sanity, not primary result)\n- WikiText-2 perplexity for FP16 vs each arm; **gate criterion** (stated a priori): if `R-W4A4KV4` PPL within \u2264 +2.0 of FP16 PPL at the same length, the model is \"working\" and its perf numbers are interpretable; otherwise the arm is flagged \"quality-failed\" and excluded/reannotated in performance conclusions. This is a precondition check only \u2014 quality is explicitly **not** a primary finding.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Phase separation and metrics\n\n### 3.1 Prefill\n- Definition: single forward pass over the whole prompt (parallel tokens; KV cache freshly written; compute-bound GEMMs with M=L).\n- Metrics: **prefill time (ms) at fixed L**; **prefill tokens/s = L / prefill_time**; **TTFT** = time-to-first-token = elapsed from request start to first generated token (\u2248 prefill time + first decode step; reported both as prefill-only and full TTFT definition, so readers can reconcile).\n\n### 3.2 Decode\n- Definition: autoregressive token-by-token loop after prefill (M=1 GEMMs; weight + growing-KV memory traffic dominate; KV read scales with generated context).\n- Metrics: **decode latency per token** (median ms/token) and **decode tokens/s**, unbatched (batch=1, primary) and **small batch=8** (secondary throughput regime); KV-cache growth tracked with context length (see \u00a74.4).\n\n### 3.3 End-to-end composite\n- Fixed task: prefill(L=4096) + decode(128 tokens), greedy; reports **total wall-clock latency** and **peak memory** across the whole run (per arm per model).\n\n### 3.4 Memory footprint accounting (arithmetic planning estimates, *not* measured results)\n- Weights FP16 \u2248 2 bytes/param: 7B \u2248 14 GB, 13B \u2248 26 GB, 70B \u2248 140 GB. Weights 4-bit \u2248 0.5 bytes/param: \u2248 3.5 / 6.5 / 35 GB.\n- KV cache per token (FP16